WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.070
Welcome to Commodity Markets Decoder.

2
00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:07.793
In the next two minutes you will see why one word, the price of oil,

3
00:00:07.793 --> 00:00:14.045
can mean four different numbers, and how to read each of them before you compare anything.

4
00:00:15.445 --> 00:00:20.380
A commodity price can be a spot assessment, a futures settlement,

5
00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.949
a monthly average of world prices, or an index.

6
00:00:23.949 --> 00:00:28.658
They differ in where, when and in what unit they are measured.

7
00:00:28.658 --> 00:00:34.429
WTI at Cushing and Brent in Europe are different crudes in different places,

8
00:00:34.429 --> 00:00:36.479
so their prices can differ.

9
00:00:37.879 --> 00:00:39.102
Units come next.

10
00:00:39.102 --> 00:00:44.378
Crude oil is quoted per barrel, and a barrel is forty-two US gallons.

11
00:00:44.378 --> 00:00:47.360
If diesel costs three dollars a gallon,

12
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.336
that is one hundred and twenty-six dollars a barrel.

13
00:00:51.336 --> 00:00:58.829
Only after converting can you compare it with crude at eighty dollars: a gap of forty-six dollars.

14
00:01:00.229 --> 00:01:02.045
Futures contracts expire.

15
00:01:02.045 --> 00:01:07.278
A continuous chart joins one contract to the next, and the join matters.

16
00:01:07.278 --> 00:01:13.600
If an expiring contract closes at sixty dollars and the next trades at sixty-one fifty,

17
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:19.923
joining them without adjustment shows a two and a half percent jump that nobody earned.

18
00:01:21.323 --> 00:01:25.553
Lining the contracts up by delivery month shows the curve.

19
00:01:25.553 --> 00:01:30.949
When later months are priced above the nearest, the market is in contango,

20
00:01:30.949 --> 00:01:34.450
and rolling a long position tends to cost money.

21
00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:38.388
When later months are cheaper, it is in backwardation.

22
00:01:39.788 --> 00:01:42.225
Prices can even fall below zero.

23
00:01:42.225 --> 00:01:51.135
On the twentieth of April 2020 the EIA’s WTI spot price was minus thirty-six dollars and ninety-eight cents a barrel.

24
00:01:51.135 --> 00:01:56.162
A percentage return across a zero or negative price means nothing,

25
00:01:56.162 --> 00:02:02.102
so ATLAS leaves such returns out and says so, rather than averaging them away.

26
00:02:03.502 --> 00:02:11.274
Commodity Markets Decoder works through each of these ideas with real benchmark series stored in ATLAS,

27
00:02:11.274 --> 00:02:13.387
worked examples and quizzes.

28
00:02:13.387 --> 00:02:17.085
None of the global benchmarks is an Indian price.

29
00:02:17.085 --> 00:02:20.707
This course is education, not investment advice.

